WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:00.900
Hey Crazies.

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:04.720
There are 3 big misconceptions about the universe
people still don’t get.

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:06.600
Let’s set the record straight!

00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:12.560
This episode was supported by Billiant.org

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:16.960
OK, so I have briefly mentioned some of these things in previous videos.

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:22.520
There’s a whole playlist on cosmology,
but I’m spastic sometimes, so I could believe

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:23.580
you might have missed them.

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:24.460
One!

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:28.180
How much bigger will I be at 80-years-old
after you account for the expansion of the

00:00:28.180 --> 00:00:29.060
universe?

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:31.340
Exactly the same size.

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:33.800
Well, maybe not exactly the same size.

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:37.700
Most people are shorter when they’re older
because of biological changes.

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:42.100
But the expansion of the space has exactly
zero effect on your size.

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:44.840
Are you saying the universe isn’t expanding
inside us?

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:45.780
Oh, it is!

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:47.640
It just isn’t strong enough to stretch you.

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:48.880
Think of it like this.

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:51.780
Imagine you and the love of your life are
holding hands,

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:56.340
but you’re each standing on your own conveyor
belts that move in opposite directions.

00:00:56.340 --> 00:00:58.980
You know, because the universe is always against
you.

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:03.560
Anyway, the belts try to pull you apart, but
they’re not strong enough to overcome the

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:05.680
grip you have on each other’s hands.

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:08.880
You stay together as the belts move beneath
you.

00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:11.820
The force from your hands holds you together.

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:15.220
The same kind of thing happens with space
except in 3 dimensions.

00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:19.520
Space stretches through you while you stay
the same size

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:24.060
because there are forces between your atoms
and molecules that hold you together.

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:27.500
They’re strong enough to overcome the expansion
of space.

00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:32.620
Solar systems, galaxies, and even clusters
of galaxies are held together by gravity,

00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:34.120
so they don’t expand either.

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:35.080
However!

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:38.780
The expansion of space is uniform throughout
the universe,

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:41.100
but gravity gets weaker with distance.

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:46.960
At a sufficiently large distance, the expansion
wins and can start spreading things out.

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:50.000
That happens between super-clusters of galaxies.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:52.890
That’s clusters of clusters of galaxies!

00:01:52.890 --> 00:01:57.770
The universe is big enough that one point
of light in this image is a super-cluster

00:01:57.770 --> 00:01:59.000
of galaxies.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:00.440
Space is big.

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:01.500
Really big.

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:02.340
Two!

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:04.660
Why does it look like we’re the center of
the universe?

00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:07.740
Because we are the center
of the observable universe.

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:12.700
We can’t see the whole entire universe because
light doesn’t travel fast enough.

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:17.140
The universe might be infinite, it might be
finite, but either way,

00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:20.700
Light hasn’t had enough time to travel all
the way across it,

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:23.540
so this is all we get to see of it.

00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:25.480
That’s the observable universe.

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:29.900
That’s how far light could have possibly
traveled since the big bang.

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:32.900
And, this is the light that’s traveled the
farthest:

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:35.090
the Cosmic Microwave Background.

00:02:35.090 --> 00:02:39.080
The observable universe and the entire universe
are not the same thing.

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:43.300
Unfortunately, some people call both
of these things “the universe”

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:45.080
and it causes a lot of confusion.

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:45.880
Anyway!

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:50.220
Since light takes the same amount of time
to travel from every direction,

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:54.380
we can see the same distance in every direction,
which makes us look like the center of the

00:02:54.380 --> 00:02:55.560
universe.

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:56.660
Even though we aren’t.

00:02:56.660 --> 00:02:57.220
Three!

00:02:57.220 --> 00:02:59.260
How can a 3D universe be flat?

00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:00.080
It’s not!

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:01.620
It’s not actually flat!

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:04.260
You know how words can have multiple meanings?

00:03:04.260 --> 00:03:09.220
When we say the universe is flat, we’re
not using the word in an ordinary way.

00:03:09.220 --> 00:03:13.600
These shapes are just visual representations
of math.

00:03:13.610 --> 00:03:16.540
“Flat” just means “Zero Curvature.”

00:03:16.540 --> 00:03:19.520
The universe doesn’t actually look like
any of these shapes.

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:23.260
Honestly, we should probably stop using them,
but people like pictures.

00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:28.340
If you want to understand anything involving
general relativity, then remember this:

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:32.420
The easiest way to see curvature is by drawing
parallel lines.

00:03:32.420 --> 00:03:36.560
For example, if you draw parallel lines on
a flat piece of paper

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:40.900
those lines will stay parallel across the
entire piece of paper.

00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:44.780
If we live in a flat universe and the
data suggests that we do,

00:03:44.780 --> 00:03:48.180
then parallel lines in our universe should
do the same thing.

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:50.080
Let’s say there are two spaceships.

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:55.320
Each ship plots out a course they think will
be parallel to the other ship’s course.

00:03:55.320 --> 00:04:00.540
If the universe is flat, those courses will
stay parallel as the ships travel.

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:04.760
No matter how far they go, they’ll always
be the same distance apart.

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:06.180
What other option is there?

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:07.920
Oh! Oh! I'm glad you asked!

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:12.700
On a sphere like the Earth, two parallel lines
come closer together over time.

00:04:12.700 --> 00:04:16.579
If the spaceships are flying in a positive
curvature universe,

00:04:16.580 --> 00:04:19.620
then their paths will come closer together
over time.

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:23.820
On a saddle, two parallel lines spread apart
over time.

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:27.380
If the spaceships are flying in a negative
curvature universe,

00:04:27.380 --> 00:04:30.140
then their paths will spread apart over time.

00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:35.300
Planes, spheres, and saddles are just lower-dimensional
examples of curvature.

00:04:35.300 --> 00:04:38.080
It’s the curvature that matters, not the
shape.

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:39.680
So I hope that clears things up!

